  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCurrent visible satellite imagery shows the development of cumulus \nclouds over much of the central U.P. due to diurnal heating. \nAdditional cloud cover has developed over the eastern U.P., thanks \nto instability over Lake Superior which has also led to a few \nscattered lake-effect rain showers. Temperatures tonight will be \nnear-normal in the mid-to-low 30s with partly cloudy skies and light \nwesterly winds. An area of surface high pressure is expected to \nbuild across the NE CONUS and Great Lakes region tonight through \nFriday as a longwave 500mb ridge progresses into the area. This area \nof high pressure will result in slightly above-normal highs Friday, \nalong with light winds shifting from northerly to southerly and \nmostly clear skies. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the surface high pressure area over the NE \nCONUS continues to build and shift eastward while a Clipper Low \nsplits over the northern Mountain West and moves into the Northern \nPlains. This setup will result in a tightly packed surface pressure \ngradient over the northern Midwest and strong WAA into the Great \nLakes region. Combined with mostly clear skies, these southerly \nwinds are likely (60-80% chance) to cause highs exceeding 80 degrees \nin the interior west over the weekend. The eastern U.P. will see \nhighs limited to the mid-70s as the southerly flow will be moderated \nby Lake Michigan surface temperatures in the 50s and 60s. Elevated \nto critical fire weather conditions are possible over the weekend, \nparticularity in the interior west. RHs will fall below 40% in the \ninterior west both Saturday and Sunday afternoons, with some areas \npossibly reaching below 35%. Overnight RH is expected to remain at \nor below 70% overnight for the western U.P. Gusts up to 30mph are \npossible for the interior west Saturday, and could be locally higher \nin areas prone to downsloping. Wind speeds will be somewhat lower \nfor Sunday, but gusts could still reach 25mph. Fire weather partners \nand the public should continue to monitor the forecast for fire \nweather concerns this weekend due to potentially elevated to \ncritical fire weather and ongoing drought conditions. \n\nOur next best chance of precipitation is predicted to arrive late \nMonday into Tuesday as a shortwave trough associated with an eastern \nCanadian low moves across the area along with a cold front. \nNortherly flow behind the front could also result in lake effect \nprecipitation chances through the early workweek as seasonably cool \ntemperatures return.",
